SEMAPHORE SEMANTICS

As of 2006/06/20 external shared memory semaphores have different
semantics than scratchpad memories semaphores.  Both have strenghts
and weakneses:

- Shared memory semaphores are meant to be used as locks. They do not
  increment or decrement. They can be used to store any value but it
  would be pretty useless.  The semantics is as follows:

  * Read: Writes 1 if there was a 0. Returns the previous value.

  * Write: Simply writes whatever value into the semaphore.

  The implementation of standard Dikjstra semaphores require a shared
  memory counter and one of these semaphores as a lock.  Wait and
  signal operations require both at least 2 reads and 1 write.

  On the positive side locks implemented with these semaphores do not
  need to be initialized.

- Scratchpad memory semaphores implement the full semantics of a
  Dikjstra semaphore and provide the aditional ability to interrupt
  their parent core.  The semantics is as follows:

  * Read: If (data != 0) decrement; else suspend_flag = 1.
	  Returns the previous value.

  * Write: If (data == 0) increment; else writes data - 1.
	   If suspend_flag interrupt_core, suspend_flag = 0.
    
  If they are used for the implementation of standard locks, they must
  be initialized to 1 by writing a 2. This may be inconvenient since
  it would require initialization-free barriers. Current practice
  seems to be busy-waiting for a specific magic value on certain
  shared memory location.


PREEMPTIVE SEMAPHORES

As of 2006/06/21 current ss_semaphores.h makes a few assumptions that
are a bit optimistic:

- They assume a single task may be blocked on a given semaphore.  This
  is too restrictive, specially for multi-threaded applications.  It
  is way too common to implement at least a two worker threads scheme.
  While one thread is processing data the other is waiting for new
  data. This scheme improves latencies even on a single processor.

- The task waiting for the semaphore is resumed as soon as the
  semaphore interrupts the processor core.  The semantics of this
  operation will actually depend on the scheduler implementation.  If
  a resumed task is given the CPU inmediately then it may be a cause
  of starvation.

Both problems are actually easier (and I believe more efficient too)
to avoid than it is to implement this semantics.

A task blocked on a semaphore just needs to yield the CPU (using
sleep(0), pthread_yield, or whatever the underlying OS provides).
There is no need to keep a table since the OS (or the thread
scheduler) already maintain such a table.

When the interrupt arrives there is no need to search for the precise
process, task or thread blocked on each semaphore.  We just need to
invoke the scheduler (this is automatic in most OSs for every incoming
interrupt) and signal foreign cores somehow.  The scheduler does not
need to give control to the task waiting for that semaphore but this
is indeed a desirable property for two reasons:

- The scheduler should be aware of current tasks priorities.  If you
  want the CPU as soon as possible you should ask for it, setting the
  maximum priority or even busy-waiting for the signaling condition.

- There is no need to mask off the IRQ inside the interrupt handler.
  Therefore you reduce the latency of realtime operations.

From the point of view of the implementation it is even more
maintainable since the preemptive version is identical to the
non-preemptive version.  It is also easier to generalize the Que
library to any supported OS or concurrency framework.
